===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q35872}}A [[craft]] used for [[transportation]] of goods, fishing, racing, recreational cruising, or military use on or in the [[water]], [[propelled]] by [[oar]]s or [[outboard motor]] or inboard [[motor]] or by [[wind]].
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=II|text=Carried somehow, somewhither, for some reason, on these surging floods, were these travelers,{{nb...}}. Even such a '''boat''' as the ''Mount Vernon'' offered a total deck space so cramped as to leave secrecy or privacy well out of the question, even had the motley and democratic assemblage of passengers been disposed to accord either.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=8|passage=Philander went into the next room{{...}}and came back with a salt mackerel{{...}}. Next he put the mackerel in a fry-pan, and the shanty began to smell like a Banks '''boat''' just in from a v'yage.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21582516-worlds-thirst-oil-could-be-nearing-peak-bad-news-producers-excellent Yesterday’s fuel]
|passage=The dawn of the oil age was fairly recent. Although the stuff was used to waterproof '''boats''' in the Middle East 6,000 years ago, extracting it in earnest began only in 1859 after an oil strike in Pennsylvania. The first barrels of crude fetched $18 (around $450 at today’s prices).}}
# {{lb|en|poker slang}} A [[full house]].
# A [[conveyance]], [[utensil]], or [[dish]] somewhat resembling a boat in shape.
#: {{co|en|a stone '''boat'''; &emsp; a gravy '''boat'''}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A large and heavy [[car#Noun|car]]; {{ng|the term connotes wasteful size}}.
#: {{nearsyn|en|land yacht#Noun|sled#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|He claimed to be broke but he always drove that ridiculous '''boat'''.}}
# {{lb|en|organic compound|physical chemistry}} One of two possible [[conformation]]s of [[cyclohexane]] rings (the other being [[chair]]), shaped roughly like a boat.
# {{lb|en|Australian politics|informal}} The refugee boats arriving in Australian waters, and by extension, refugees generally.
#: {{rfquote-sense|en}}
# {{lb|en|cellular automata}} In Conway’s [[Game of Life]], a particular [[still life]] consisting of a dead [[cell]] surrounded by five living cells.
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=1994-05-07|author=David Bell|title=HighLife - An Interesting Variant of Life (part 1/3)|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/aetKFg0hths/m/QAPO3B6QoFsJ|text=It creates 4 blocks, a '''boat''', and a glider every 768 generations.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=2004-05-24|author=Paul Chapman|title=A Prototype Programmable Universal Constructor for Conway's Life|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/xiyIszapF8o/m/X_bPNmDH2ucJ|text=The program is represented as a string of '''boats''' (1s) and blocks (0s).}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=2005-02-23|author=Dave Greene|title=exist glider gun able of reconstruction in Life?|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/Iw1hF3RcL9Q/m/-wURpW8ZzjMJ|text=For many stable patterns, by the way, there are other input glider lanes where the gliders are caught and turned into '''boats''', which are then cleanly deleted by another glider coming in on the same lane.}}
# {{alternative form of|en|BOAT}}

====Usage notes====
* There is no explicit limit, but the word ''boat'' usually refers to a relatively small watercraft, smaller than a [[ship]] but larger than a [[dinghy]]. It is also the normal designation for a submarine (however large), and also for [[laker]]s (ships used in the [[Great Lakes]] trade in North America).

